timeshift-on-demand (0.1.0~dev7) UNRELEASED; urgency=low

  * Fixed a real false-negative on the login/resume auto-prompt and the
    Backup tab: the drive-mount wait budget (6 attempts, 5s apart = 30s)
    was too short for a USB enclosure recovering from an unclean
    suspend-unmount. Confirmed via journalctl -k evidence on real
    hardware across three separate resume events: this drive throws a
    mid-suspend I/O error and aborted ext4 journal, then needs ext4
    journal recovery on resume before it's mountable again -- observed
    taking anywhere from 5s to 129s. Companion's wait was giving up
    before recovery finished, most narrowly by just 4 seconds on one
    occasion. Bumped the wait budget to 36 attempts (180s total) in both
    the GTK app's backup_runner.py and the standalone
    timeshift-on-demand-prompt script; documented the distinction
    between this (self-recovering, just slow) and the previously known
    dead-needs-replug USB failure in docs/troubleshooting.md.
  * Documentation fixes from a fresh-eyes review of the new docs/ set:
    corrected a copy-paste path mismatch in installation.md's install
    command (was `./`, should be `../`, matching README.md); clarified
    that `timeshift` itself is a declared .deb dependency, not a manual
    prerequisite; introduced "Companion" as this app's short name on
    first use in README.md instead of using it unexplained; noted in
    usage.md that the Maintenance tab's "Fix Scheduling" button doesn't
    visually change when it's correctly refusing to act as a no-op.

