timeshift-on-demand (0.1.0~dev5) UNRELEASED; urgency=low

  * Fixed a second real bug in the resume-from-suspend hook, found via
    real testing right after the dev4 fix: pkexec failed ("Error
    creating textual authentication agent... exit 127") when confirming
    the backup, because `systemd-run --uid=` (dev4's fix) runs the
    prompt as a plain root-owned system service that isn't actually a
    member of the user's logind session -- pkexec couldn't find the
    graphical polkit agent to use. Switched to launching through the
    user's own `systemd --user` manager instead (`systemd-run --user
    --machine="$user@.host"`), which keeps the process genuinely inside
    the user's session-adjacent unit tree without reintroducing the
    original PAM-session-kill problem.
  * Verified end-to-end for real: pkexec authorized correctly, a genuine
    `timeshift --create` ran to completion (RSYNC snapshot saved,
    tagged, "Backup completed successfully"), no stray processes left
    behind. The full resume-hook -> confirm -> authorize -> backup ->
    completion pipeline is now confirmed working, not just each piece
    individually.
  * Replaced the terminal-based live monitor (gnome-terminal/xterm
    spawning a bash script) with a small standalone GTK window
    (timeshift-on-demand-gui-monitor). Genuine simplification, not just
    cosmetic: drops the gnome-terminal/xterm dependency entirely.
    Verified locally: streams log lines live, auto-closes on success
    with the doneflag cleaned up, stays open indefinitely on failure.

